 Ordinary Differential Equations 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

It is probably more familiar if we use 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, and if we show the independent variable, say t, explicitly: 
\begin_inset Formula \[a_n\frac{d^ny}{dt^n}+\cdots +a_1\frac{dy}{dt}+a_0y =
 b_m\frac{d^mu}{dt^m}+\cdots + b_1\frac{du}{dt}+b_0u\]
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}\label{diffeq}
\diffeq
\end{equation}
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

For our purposes, the function 
\begin_inset Formula $u=u(t)$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\shape italic

\series bold
input
\series default

\shape default
, while the function 
\begin_inset Formula $y=y(t)$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\shape italic

\series bold
output
\series default

\shape default
. The independent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is thought of as time. 
\end_layout

