 Ordinary Differential Equations 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

We have used 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 rather than 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 as in most physics books, because we want to be consistent with 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \eqref{diffeq}

\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

It is common to rewrite such equations so that the leading coefficient is 1. Thus we have: 
\begin_inset Formula \[\frac{d^2y}{dt^2}+\frac{k}{m}y=0.\]
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

It is easily verified that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sin\omega_0 t$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_0=\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}$
\end_inset

, satisfies this differential equation. 
\end_layout

