\end_layout

\end_inset

 Ordinary Differential Equations 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

This double linearity means, on the one hand, that the output 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 due to several inputs acting at the same time is equal to the sum of the outputs due to each input acting alone. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

On the other hand, it also means that if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_2$
\end_inset

 are both solutions to the homogeneous version of 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \eqref{diffeq}

\end_inset

, then so is 
\begin_inset Formula $c_1y_1+c_2y_2$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_2$
\end_inset

 are any real constants. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

A maximal set of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 such solutions to the homogeneous equation that are also linearly independent (as vectors) is called a 
\shape italic

\series bold
fundamental set
\series default

\shape default
. 
\end_layout

