 Ordinary Differential Equations 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

You can find a fundamental set by solving the 
\shape italic

\series bold
characteristic equation
\series default

\shape default
 associated with the differential equation. This equation is nothing other than the original polynomial equation in 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

, but with 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 replaced by an unknown such as 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

Thus, the characteristic equation for 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \eqref{diffeq}

\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula \[a_nz^n+\cdots + a_1z + a_0=0\]
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

We use 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 because the roots of this equation are in general complex (thanks, once again, to Dr. Gauss). 
\end_layout

